  "summary": "Frozen pathology foundation models are being fused, calibrated and tuned to reduce slide-scanner dependence in biomarker prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that evaluation in computational pathology is shifting from discrimination toward the reliability of model outputs. CALIFUSE treats disagreement among several frozen pathology foundation models as a basis for calibrated uncertainty in whole-slide image classification, using representational disagreement and Jensen-Shannon chunk agreement. HERMES addresses interpretability of tumor immune microenvironment reasoning from paired H&E and mIF by applying a process reward that checks stated quantities against cell-level truth, rather than scoring only final answers. The tempering fine-tuning study targets slide-scanner dependence, reporting improved digital biomarker reproducibility across HPV, MSI, prostate grading and breast tasks after few-shot calibration. What is new is the focus on intermediate reasoning and cross-model or cross-scanner consistency. All three items are preprints, so these findings remain provisional.",
